# ── gen491 (2026-09-01) — PERCHE' LE ATTESE «Learned: pred(arg)» SONO CAMBIATE ─
#
# Questo file asseriva la NOTAZIONE come risposta attesa: «Learned: man(socrates).»
# Un ratchet che congela un difetto lo difende con l'autorita' di un verde, ed
# era il caso: la risposta principale di parrot0 non era LINGUA, e l'audit
# dell'eco (scripts/self_echo_audit.py) ha mostrato che rileggendosi parrot0
# chiamava la propria notazione «a snippet of code» — non la riconosceva come
# propria.
#
# Le attese sono state ritirate, non allineate a un comportamento qualunque: la
# sostituzione e' avvenuta SOLO dove il vecchio atteso era un termine e il nuovo
# e' la frase corrispondente. L'invariante che regge il cambio sta in
# tests/p0t/conversation/self_echo.p0t — tutto cio' che parrot0 dice deve
# essergli comprensibile — e adesso la conferma e' un punto fisso:
#
#     > a whale is a mammal      →  Learned: whale is a mammal.
#     > whale is a mammal        →  Learned: whale is a mammal.
#
# migrated from tests/cases/prosefact.chat (gen346, hermetic + isolated)
[mock hermetic]
!set PARROT0_BASE=
!set PARROT0_SESSION=
!set PARROT0_WORLD_FACTS=0
!set PARROT0_LANG=en
!reset

[test prosefact]
# Generation 296 (deep-reasoning M0, comprehension frame 1): a leading DETERMINER on
# the subject of a class statement — the way Wikipedia lead sentences and ordinary
# prose phrase a membership fact. First step of prose->fact extraction
# (docs/plans/deep-reasoning.md §4): the SAME comprehension parser that stores a
# user's "X is a Y" now also digests "A whale is a mammal". Hermetic base, so every
# fact here is genuinely learned, not recalled.

# Leading article on the subject, as an assertion.
> A whale is a mammal
< Learned: whale is a mammal.
# ...and queried back with the article too (verb-first).
> is a whale a mammal?
< Yes.
# "The" + "an" variants.
> The dog is an animal
< Learned: dog is an animal.
> is the dog an animal?
< Yes.
# The payoff for deep reasoning: an extracted membership feeds a MULTI-HOP category
# chain (whale -> mammal -> vertebrate) over a universal rule.
> every mammal is a vertebrate
< Learned rule: vertebrate(X) :- mammal(X).
> is a whale a vertebrate?
< Yes.
# Regression: a bare subject (no article) still stores the membership as before.
> zorb is a blit
< Learned: zorb is a blit.

# gen298 (frame 2): PAST-TENSE copula "was/were", as Wikipedia phrases historical
# subjects. Tenseless membership fact; combines with the leading article of frame 1.
> Socrates was a philosopher
< Learned: socrates is a philosopher.
> is socrates a philosopher?
< Yes.
> A dinosaur was an animal
< Learned: dinosaur is an animal.
> is a dinosaur an animal?
< Yes.
# The NOUN "era" is not mistaken for a copula (no article follows), so this stays
# an honest wall rather than a misparse.
> the victorian era was long
<~ Hmm, I don't know about victorian yet. Want me to learn about it?

# gen299 (frame 3): MULTI-WORD subject and class join into one atom.
> The blue whale is a marine mammal
< Learned: blue whale is a marine mammal.
# gen299 (frame 4): a trailing PREPOSITIONAL PHRASE yields TWO facts from one
# sentence — the class AND a located_in edge (the seed of geographic multi-hop).
> France is a country in Europe
< Learned: france is a country, located_in(france, europe).
# gen299 (frame 6): locative frames — "is located in", bare "is in", "is part of".
> France is located in Europe
< Learned: france is located in europe.
> Sicily is part of Italy
< Learned: sicily is part of italy.
# A predicate adjective (no article) is NOT a membership: honest wall, not long(x).
> the sky is blue
< I don't understand that yet.

# gen300 (frame 5): a CONJUNCTION of article-bearing classes -> one fact each; with
# a trailing PP, all in one sentence.
> A dolphin is a mammal and a swimmer
< Learned: dolphin is a mammal, dolphin is a swimmer.
> France is a country and a republic in Europe
< Learned: france is a country, france is a republic, located_in(france, europe).
# The relational conjunction ("the capital and largest city of France") has no
# article on the second conjunct, so it is left alone rather than mis-extracted.
> Paris is the capital and largest city of France
<~ Hmm, I don't know about capital yet. Want me to learn about it?

